docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:213:Source: `runs/runnerup_analysis.json`, `<family>.<version>.runner_up_minus_r3.markets`; my equal-weight means, sign counts and t calculations. The final column agrees with `.cstar_minus_runner_up.estimate`.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:219:A small inferential clarification would help. Run A applies the same materiality margin to nominal percentile intervals. Its direction label does not use the registered calibration and Holm procedure. This is disclosed in the plan and analysis file, but should be stated beside the paper’s quoted category. Source: `runs/drivers/common.py::q1_category`; `runs/runner-up-plan.md`, §3.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:221:### Run B
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:242:## 6. Structure and length
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:264:The novelty should be stated narrowly. Credibility-based checking with identity replacement already appears in Sarmenta’s work, and BOINC’s adaptive replication uses verified history to vary checking. The paper acknowledges this correctly. Its contribution is the controlled comparison and the observed failure under honest turnover, rather than the basic idea of checking newcomers. [groups.csail.mit.edu](https://groups.csail.mit.edu/cag/bayanihan/papers/ccgrid01/ccgrid01.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:266:I checked the short arguments for Lemma 1, Corollary 1 and the conditional renewal bound under their stated assumptions. I did not find an algebraic error. Their assumptions, especially those of the renewal bound, do not establish the behaviour of the implemented finite-horizon process. The paper now says that explicitly.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:268:## 7. New problems and exact fixes
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:270:### 1. The reference-reselection diagnostic is described too broadly
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:282:### 2. Run B’s cell selection is factually misdescribed
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:294:### 3. The conclusion blurs registered calibration and exploratory intervals
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:306:### 4. The abstract combines two different low-rate influence patterns
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:316:### 5. The theory remark gives an overly broad zero-coverage condition
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:318:> “with honest churn it shares the newest positions with honest newcomers, and the bound is zero whenever a newer identity holds jobs or the quota is already spent” (p.7).
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:320:A newer identity holding a job does not itself force coverage to zero.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:322:For a constructed counterexample, take a residual quota of ten audits. Five go to newest-first targeting. Suppose a newer established identity has one job and the focal next-newest identity has two. Both focal jobs still receive targeted audits. These are counterexample inputs, not simulation results.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:326:> “With honest churn, the uniquely-newest guarantee may no longer apply. Coverage can be zero if newer jobs exhaust the relevant quota.”
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:330:### 6. Two short scope corrections remain in the conclusion and abstract
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:352:### 7. The response incorrectly says the newest-identity statistic needs another run
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:354:> “The p = 0 diagnostic row and the newest-identity frequency (both need a diagnostics pass).”  
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:361:Add the existing newest-identity frequencies, with their denominator defined, and change the response to say that only the zero-churn allocation baseline still needs a diagnostics pass.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:363:For release preparation, also repair the hard-coded test path to `runnerup_analysis.json` and include the Run A raw records needed to replay its intervals. These are reproducibility improvements; the missing records do not demonstrate that the reported result is wrong.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:365:### A useful additional analysis: deterioration versus reversal
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:399:These intervals are post hoc, unadjusted and based on few markets. They omit search uncertainty, and the nominal exclusions of zero should not be promoted into a new confirmatory claim. The complete rate table should be reported if this analysis is added.
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:405:## 8. Decision
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:411:| **WEIS 2027** | **3/5, upper borderline** | **50%** | Medium | A prospectively specified churn replication on new market draws, including both C* and the runner-up, with sufficient continued search to assess the cells that determine the result. |
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:413:| **ACM EC** | **1/5** | **3%** | High | A substantive theorem about achievable or unavoidable incentive costs under free replacement and honest turnover. The current conditional renewal theorem does not establish such a result for the implemented mechanism. |
docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md:415:For WEIS, I would now treat this as a credible but narrow empirical paper with a useful negative result. Its numerical reporting is no longer the main obstacle. The highest-value next step is to test how far the churn result extends, with the design fixed before seeing the new outcomes.

